If  $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$  has no real roots, then its discriminant is negative:  $b^2 - 4ac < 0$ , or  $c > (b^2)/(4a)$ .  Hence,  $a > 0$  in order to ensure zero real roots, or else we have a contradiction otherwise.  Let  $c > -(a + b)$  (i) such that: $-(a + b) = \frac{b^2}{4a}$ ;

