By **Lemma 13** [here](https://web.math.pmf.unizg.hr/~duje/pdf/bound.pdf), if  $a<b<c$  and all of  $ab+1.bc+1,ca+1$  are perfect squares, then either  $c = a + b + 2 \sqrt{ab+1}$  or  $c \ge 4ab$ . If  $a,b,c$  form an arithmetic progression, then the second possibility cannot occur, and the first (together with the AP condition) then implies that  $b$  is even and  $y^2 - 3(b/2)^2 = 1$ , where  $y=b-a=c-b$ . Hence all such triples come from the Pell equation.

This implies that there are no arithmetic progressions of length  $4$  satisfying a similar condition.

Let the consecutive +ve integers be (r+1), (r+2)......(r+n)
Then we have
 $ (r\plus{}1)(r\plus{}2)(r\plus{}3).......(r\plus{}n) 
\equal{}\frac{r!(r\plus{}1)(r\plus{}2)(r\plus{}3).......(r\plus{}n)}{r!}
\equal{}\frac{(r\plus{}n)!}{r!}
\equal{}\frac{(r\plus{}n)!n!}{r!n!}
\equal{}\frac{(n\plus{}r)!n!}{n![(n\plus{}r)\minus{}n]}
\equal{}C(n\plus{}r,n)n!$ 


Hence the result!

